url: https://mskazemi.com/m100-silicon-binning/
abstract: >-
  Vendors sell large processor dies as lower-core-count parts by disabling
  cores, defective or not, without saying which. On CINECA's Marconi100
  supercomputer the baseboard management controller exposes one thermal sensor
  per core position without renumbering survivors, so the positions that never
  report form each die's harvest map. From public telemetry we recover maps for
  1,962 POWER9 sockets over 1.67 million socket-days. In every map the eight
  disabled cores of the 24-core die form four complete slices, core pairs
  sharing an L2/L3 block, although the hardware can disable single cores; 443
  of 495 possible patterns occur, varying from die to die. Disabled slices
  cluster above all within the six quads, slice pairs adjacent in IBM's chip
  diagram, where thermal coupling across 1,948 sockets is also strongest. A
  sharp boundary at rack 22, which places 47 of 49 held-out racks in the
  right lot, separates two populations of parts with different disable statistics, most plausibly
  procurement lots. An independent boot signal places a boot inside 133 of 136
  steady-state map changes and never shows a change without one. Nine
  steady-state core-guard episodes, removing a slice or core at boot, are each
  matched by the operating system's own CPU count, whose window brackets the
  sensor one. Maps travel with their dies, so map
  changes trace processors between nodes: sockets change die at 2.1% per year,
  but new processors enter at an inferred 1.0-1.2%. An equivalence analysis
  bounds the map's association with socket power to a few watts, below the
  spread between nominally identical sockets, and a survival model finds no
  useful prediction of which dies get replaced.
  The disclosure lies in which sensors exist, not their values, so coarser
  sampling does not remove it.
keywords:
  - silicon harvesting
  - core harvesting
  - silicon binning
  - hardware provenance
  - POWER9
  - HPC telemetry
  - out-of-band monitoring
  - baseboard management controller
  - semiconductor yield
  - side channel
  - Marconi100
  - reproducible research
